 {\"typing\", \"typing_extensions\"} or has_origin:\n    return self._to_typing(raw, of_type, factory)\n```\n\nThis allows Python 3.9+ built-in generic type syntax to work alongside the existing `typing` module types.\n\n### 2. New Tests in `tests/config/test_sets.py`\n\nAdded four new tests to validate the functionality:\n- `test_config_list_parameterized` - Tests `list[str]` type\n- `test_config_set_parameterized` - Tests `set[int]` type\n- `test_config_dict_parameterized` - Tests `dict[str, int]` type\n- `test_config_optional_parameterized` - Tests `Optional[int]` type\n\n### 3. Changelog Entry in `docs/changelog/parameterized-generics.feature.rst`\n\nDocumented the feature:\n> Support parameterized generic types (e.g., ``list[str]``, ``set[int]``, ``dict[str, int]``) in ``ConfigSet.add_config`` ``of_type`` parameter, allowing use of Python 3.9+ built-in generic type syntax instead of ``typing`` module imports.\n\n## Verification\n\nAll 1059 existing config tests pass, and all 4 new tests for parameterized generics also pass. The change is backward compatible and works seamlessly with existing type handling for simple types.","ttft_ms":1108,"type":"result","duration_ms":39788,"uuid":"bce1a0f8-54e9-4541-9aa4-58a1eaccaca7","ttft_stream_ms":1107,"time_to_request_ms":65,"queued_turn_count":0}
